# Inline and supervised Nx.Serving over an encoded batch

## Goal

Run the same row-wise function over a `wotex_nx` encoded batch twice: once
inline with `Nx.Serving.run/2`, once through a supervised `Nx.Serving`
process placed under an explicit Lab instance with a caller-chosen name, and
show that both paths agree and that concurrent callers get their own rows
back. The serving function is a plain Nx function; nothing here is a model.

The Lab lane `serving-batches` (WLB.03) is **partial** in source: the
supervised serving, batch keys and concurrent correlation run below, while
the acceptance items for batch padding, timeout, stop-under-load and
overload are not yet automated in the Lab test suite. This notebook does not
claim them.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.13.1"}
])
```

The requirements above are published-artifact requirements. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`docs/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Batch construction, feature order, masks, quality | `wotex_nx` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Nx.Encoded.batch/1`, `template/1` |
| Serving process, batching, keys | `nx` | `Nx.Serving` |
| Process placement, name, lifetime | consumer (`wotex_lab` instance) | `Wotex.Lab.start_child/3` |
| The serving function | consumer | the anonymous function below |

The Lab never starts a serving implicitly: the name is chosen here, the
process is placed under an instance started here, and both are stopped here.

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.Lab
alias Wotex.Lab.Examples.Thermal
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry
alias Wotex.Nx.Encoded

telemetry_id = "cookbook-serving-batches-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())
```

The encoded batch comes from the thermal foundation; `Encoded.template/1`
describes the container the serving must accept without realizing tensors.

```elixir
{:ok, thermal} = Thermal.run()
encoded = thermal.encoded
batch = Encoded.batch(encoded)
{Encoded.feature_order(encoded), Encoded.row_count(encoded), Encoded.template(encoded)}
```

A row-wise function keeps the batch axis, so `Nx.Serving` can split results
back to their callers: `value * mask + 1` per row.

```elixir
row_target = fn {{values}, {masks}, _quality} ->
  Nx.add(Nx.multiply(values, Nx.as_type(masks, Nx.type(values))), 1.0)
end

serving = Nx.Serving.jit(row_target, compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)

inline =
  Telemetry.span(:nx, :inference, %{profile: :serving_inline}, fn ->
    Nx.Serving.run(serving, batch)
  end)

Nx.to_flat_list(inline)
```

The supervised path: an explicit Lab instance owns the serving process under
its `:sessions` role, with a caller-chosen name and batch size.

```elixir
{:ok, lab} = Lab.start_link(id: "serving-batches", max_children: 4)
serving_name = :wotex_lab_cookbook_serving

{:ok, serving_pid} =
  Lab.start_child(
    lab,
    :sessions,
    {Nx.Serving,
     serving: serving,
     name: serving_name,
     batch_size: 4,
     batch_timeout: 10,
     batch_keys: [:default, :room_a]}
  )

supervised =
  Telemetry.span(:nx, :inference, %{profile: :serving_supervised}, fn ->
    Nx.Serving.batched_run(serving_name, batch)
  end)

{Nx.to_flat_list(supervised), Process.alive?(serving_pid)}
```

Concurrent callers each submit a one-row batch; the serving batches them
together and every caller receives exactly its own row.

```elixir
{{values}, {masks}, quality} =
  Nx.Defn.jit_apply(&Function.identity/1, [batch], compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)

one_row = fn index ->
  Nx.Batch.stack([
    {{values[index..index]}, {masks[index..index]}, quality[index..index]}
  ])
end

correlated =
  0..1
  |> Task.async_stream(fn index ->
    {index, Nx.to_flat_list(Nx.Serving.batched_run(serving_name, one_row.(index)))}
  end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, pair} -> pair end)
  |> Enum.sort()

correlated
```

Batch keys: `Nx.Batch.key/2` tags a batch so a serving can separate work
that must not be batched together; the serving declared `:room_a` next to
`:default` when it started and keeps the two apart.

```elixir
keyed = Nx.Batch.key(batch, :room_a)
keyed_result = Nx.Serving.batched_run(serving_name, keyed)
{keyed.key, Nx.to_flat_list(keyed_result)}
```

Stop the serving and the instance explicitly.

```elixir
:ok = Supervisor.stop(lab)
serving_stopped? = not Process.alive?(serving_pid) and Process.whereis(serving_name) == nil
```

## Deliberate breakage

A serving function that drops the batch axis cannot serve a batch: the
thermal `target/1` reduces two rows to one scalar, and `Nx.Serving.run/2`
refuses the result before any process is involved because it cannot split a
rank-0 output back to callers. This is a consumer contract, not a Lab rule.

```elixir
scalar_serving = Nx.Serving.jit(&Thermal.target/1, compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)

scalar_refusal =
  try do
    Nx.Serving.run(scalar_serving, batch)
  rescue
    error in ArgumentError -> {:refused, Exception.message(error)}
  end
```

## Safe telemetry

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

inference_profiles =
  for {[:wotex, :lab, :nx, :inference, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- spans,
      do: metadata.profile

labels_safe? = not (inspect(spans) =~ "21.0") and not (inspect(spans) =~ "urn:")
{inference_profiles, labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `inline-supervised-agree` | Both paths return about `[21.0, 23.0]` |
| `concurrent-correlation` | Caller 0 gets `[21.0]`, caller 1 gets `[23.0]` |
| `batch-keys` | A keyed batch carries its key and computes the same rows |
| `serving-stopped` | The serving name is free after the instance stops |

```elixir
close? = fn left, right -> abs(left - right) < 0.00001 end

agree? = fn left, right ->
  length(left) == length(right) and
    Enum.zip(left, right) |> Enum.all?(fn {a, b} -> close?.(a, b) end)
end

%{
  "inline-supervised-agree" =>
    agree?.(Nx.to_flat_list(inline), [21.0, 23.0]) and
      agree?.(Nx.to_flat_list(supervised), [21.0, 23.0]),
  "concurrent-correlation" =>
    match?([{0, [a]}, {1, [b]}] when abs(a - 21.0) < 0.00001 and abs(b - 23.0) < 0.00001, correlated),
  "batch-keys" => keyed.key == :room_a and agree?.(Nx.to_flat_list(keyed_result), [21.0, 23.0]),
  "serving-stopped" => serving_stopped? and match?({:refused, _message}, scalar_refusal) and labels_safe?
}
```

## Public seam

No WoTEx seam is implemented here. The consumer contract is the container
shape from `Wotex.Nx.Encoded.template/1`; the serving process is `nx`'s
`Nx.Serving` under the consumer's supervision tree.

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.03 `serving-batches` lane, source status partial (`WLB-C03`,
  `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `WNX-C04`, `WNX-C05`.

## Replacement adapter

Swap `Nx.Defn.Evaluator` for a compiled backend by changing the
`compiler:` option and selecting the backend in your host; keep the same
container function. To serve a real model, build the serving from that
model's predict function and validate its output through
`Wotex.Nx.Decoder.decode/3` before any policy reads it.
